				<div class="mt-24 flex flex-col gap-32">
					<!-- Arc 1: the losslessness law -->
					<article class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-6 sm:pl-10">
						<div class="font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-stamped-canary">
							ARC 1 · v1.3.0 -> v1.3.2 · THE LOSSLESSNESS LAW
						</div>
						<h3 class="mt-6 font-headline text-xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-2xl">
							Hash the ORIGINAL, never the wrapper
						</h3>
						<p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The incident.</strong> Compressing a
							Hermes wrapper stored the <em>extracted</em> content under
							<code class="font-mono text-base">hash(extracted)</code> and discarded the
							original. A failed command's <code class="font-mono text-base">exit_code</code>
							and <code class="font-mono text-base">error</code> fields were unrecoverable
							after compression, and caller JSON that merely matched a wrapper shape -
							e.g. <code class="font-mono text-base">{'{"content":"hi","id":42}'}</code> - got
							permanently collapsed to one field.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The fix.</strong> Both the hook and the
							tool paths now hash and store the ORIGINAL content always; the unwrapped
							view is used only for classification and preview.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">Mechanics today.</strong> Retrieval is
							byte-exact by construction - backends store and return content verbatim
							as UTF-8 strings, no zstd on the path - and a full-document retrieval
							(<code class="font-mono text-base">limit: 0</code>) returns the exact
							original bytes, hashing back to the marker's own hash.
						</p>
						<div class="mt-6 font-mono text-base text-zinc-dust">
							SRC: CHANGELOG.md:683-706 · docs/api/retrieve.md ("byte-exact by construction")
						</div>
					</article>
					<!-- Arc 2: the publishing incident -->
					<article class="border-l-4 border-stamped-canary pl-6 sm:pl-10">
						<div class="font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-stamped-canary">
							ARC 2 · v1.3.8 -> v1.3.9 · THE PUBLISHING INCIDENT
						</div>
						<h3 class="mt-6 font-headline text-xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-2xl">
							The tarball that compiled locally and failed everywhere else
						</h3>
						<p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The incident.</strong> v1.3.8 published to
							crates.io but was <em>uninstallable</em>: <code class="font-mono text-base">Cargo.toml</code>
							had <code class="font-mono text-base">exclude = [..., "*.md", ...]</code>, and
							cargo applies <code class="font-mono text-base">exclude</code> at publish time -
							recursively. The six <code class="font-mono text-base">builtin_directives/*.md</code>
							files embedded via <code class="font-mono text-base">include_str!</code> were
							stripped from the tarball, so the local checkout built fine and CI's Test
							job passed - but <code class="font-mono text-base">cargo install aphrodite</code>
							failed at compile time.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The fix.</strong> Drop the recursive
							<code class="font-mono text-base">*.md</code> exclusion (and never combine
							<code class="font-mono text-base">include</code> with
							<code class="font-mono text-base">exclude</code> - cargo would ignore
							<code class="font-mono text-base">exclude</code> and drop the targets), add a
							<code class="font-mono text-base">cargo package --list</code> packaging guard
							to CI, and a durable in-crate regression test that fails when
							<code class="font-mono text-base">*.md</code> returns to
							<code class="font-mono text-base">exclude</code>.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">Mechanics today.</strong> crates.io versions
							are immutable - v1.3.8 stays broken forever, superseded as the working
							release; the packaging guard means this class of regression can never
							reach crates.io again.
						</p>
						<div class="mt-6 font-mono text-base text-zinc-dust">SRC: CHANGELOG.md:408-450</div>
					</article>
					<!-- Arc 3: one runtime home -->
					<article class="border-l-4 border-weathered-teal pl-6 sm:pl-10">
						<div class="font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-weathered-teal">
							ARC 3 · v1.6.3 · ONE RUNTIME HOME
						</div>
						<h3 class="mt-6 font-headline text-xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-2xl">
							One decision, made once, by both halves
						</h3>
						<p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The incident.</strong> Issue #40: the
							plugin's Python shim resolved the runtime home from
							<code class="font-mono text-base">$HERMES_HOME</code> while the Rust half
							resolved it from <code class="font-mono text-base">$HOME</code>. Whenever
							they diverged - the shipped Docker image, profile gateways - the shim
							looked for binaries in a directory that never existed, disabled the
							plugin, and orphaned the previous runtime home, config and provider API
							key included.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The fix.</strong> The shim resolves the
							runtime home once (<code class="font-mono text-base">$APHRODITE_HOME</code>
							override, then <code class="font-mono text-base">&lt;hermes-home&gt;/aphrodite</code>,
							then <code class="font-mono text-base">~/.hermes/aphrodite</code>) and exports
							it to the Rust half through <code class="font-mono text-base">APHRODITE_HOME</code>,
							so the dylib and the spawned proxy agree by construction.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							Legacy installs are adopted with a one-line warning, never migrated.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">Mechanics today.</strong> Per-Hermes-home
							isolation extends to the whole stack, and the startup log names the
							decision: <code class="font-mono text-base">runtime home: &lt;path&gt; (decided by ...)</code>.
						</p>
						<div class="mt-6 font-mono text-base text-zinc-dust">SRC: CHANGELOG.md:104-154</div>
					</article>
					<!-- Arc 4: the checksum law -->
					<article class="border-l-4 border-raw-blood pl-6 sm:pl-10">
						<div class="font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-raw-blood">
							ARC 4 · v1.6.5 · THE CHECKSUM LAW
						</div>
						<h3 class="mt-6 font-headline text-xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-2xl">
							In-tree SHA256SUMS.txt is the only truth
						</h3>
						<p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The incident.</strong> The download scripts
							used to fetch the release-hosted per-target checksums and had
							warn-and-skip paths - a missing checksum file or tool degraded to a
							warning instead of a refusal.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							A plugin-hardening catalog review called it out.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The fix.</strong> The pinned tree's
							<code class="font-mono text-base">SHA256SUMS.txt</code> became the only
							checksum source of truth: a missing file, a
							<code class="font-mono text-base">BINARY_VERSION</code> mismatch, a missing
							target block, or a missing asset entry is a hard refusal (exit 1), and a
							missing <code class="font-mono text-base">shasum</code>/<code class="font-mono text-base">sha256sum</code>
							tool is a hard refusal rather than a skipped check.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							The legacy <code class="font-mono text-base">~/.hermes/aphrodite</code> home
							adoption in the runtime lookup is also gone.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">Mechanics today.</strong> Every release
							ships 12 artifacts - 4 targets x (binary, plugin dylib, SHA256SUMS) - and
							the dylib arm refuses installs when the in-tree checksums are missing.
						</p>
						<div class="mt-6 font-mono text-base text-zinc-dust">SRC: CHANGELOG.md:39-67 · the Aphrodite/v1.6.5 release note</div>
					</article>
					<!-- Arc 5: the path guards -->
					<article class="border-l-4 border-stamped-canary pl-6 sm:pl-10">
						<div class="font-mono text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-stamped-canary">
							ARC 5 · v1.6.6 · THE PATH GUARDS
						</div>
						<h3 class="mt-6 font-headline text-xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-2xl">
							Some paths are never read
						</h3>
						<p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The incident.</strong> Nothing stopped the
							prefetch path from reading the files most likely to contain secrets -
							environment files, auth tokens, SSH keys, or the Hermes home itself.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">The fix.</strong>
							<code class="font-mono text-base">aphrodite_prefetch</code> refuses
							<code class="font-mono text-base">.env</code>, <code class="font-mono text-base">auth.json</code>,
							<code class="font-mono text-base">~/.ssh</code>, the Hermes home, and non-file
							paths outright, answering with <code class="font-mono text-base">ReadOutcome::Refused</code>
							plus a <code class="font-mono text-base">refuse_reason</code> instead of
							reading them.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<strong class="text-bone-white">Mechanics today.</strong> Setup is hardened
							the same way: the Hermes-home plugin directory is report-only - no
							writes, no deletes - and the same release re-described the context engine
							as opt-in with a <code class="font-mono text-base">requires_hermes &gt;= 0.20.2</code>
							floor.
						</p>
						<div class="mt-6 font-mono text-base text-zinc-dust">SRC: CHANGELOG.md:3-37 · the Aphrodite/v1.6.6 release note</div>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

